 let mut text=String::from("# Sparse full-scalar coalescing: native complete-query trial\n\n**The high-core caller gate fails; no adoption.** The primary equal-log A/B2 aggregate at32workers is1.045199 with interval[1.037001,1.061052],0/15winning blocks:4.52%slower by the paired estimate. Single-worker A improves7.05%, and16-worker A improves1.10%; those gains are retained below. Primary B2 slows at every tested worker count. Production source176, accepted SDK and proof ledger27,353 remain unchanged.\n\nThe earlier [operation-count screen](../2026-10-30-bounded-coalesce176/README.md) predicted3.329%/3.104%fewer primary A/B2 generic fill/top pairs after group formation. This trial implements the proposal and measures its full query cost. Count savings alone did not predict high-core latency. This is a query experiment, not new proof or AWS latency.\n\n## Mechanism and memory\n\nReuse the original full scalar classification when its final population cutoff declines grouping. Preserve sampling, memory admission, capacity policy, stable class IDs, zeros and cutoff. The existing arrays then support1024-row tile-local grouping inside original point partitions. Combine only equal entire nonzero scalars with at least two active original digits; ignore identity operands. Packed count/representative/group tags reuse existing count records, with a2KiB u16 link array and up to512eight-byte tile records. Reduce all selected groups with the existing complete affine group kernel, then retain sorted skip rows and representative-point records. Release original classification before curve reduction. Admitted global grouping bypasses this path, as do unsupported inputs.\n\nWindows pass original contiguous runs to existing borrowed batch loops, substitute only derived repeated sums and omit their other rows. No singleton point array, retained key lookup table or duplicate solver plan is created. G2 retains original high/low flush boundaries and Phi application. Narrow tops use the original indexed reducer through a mapped getter. Width12, A's one part, B2's two parts, weighting, Horner steps, field arithmetic, inverses and exceptional arithmetic remain. The private query wrappers reconstruct window scheduling; this trial measures the resulting implementation as a whole, rather than isolating a causal effect from saved additions.\n\n| Primary query | Derived sums | Skipped rows | Retained overlay payload |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| A |227|583|18,676bytes|\n| B2 |325|849|47,596bytes|\n\nActual record alignment makes these payloads larger than the prior count screen's hypothetical17,768/46,296bytes. They exclude original inputs, classification/formation scratch, allocator overhead, stacks and all other query workspace. Optional checked reservations conservatively charge new metadata, formation workspace and possible buffer-reallocation overlap using the actual duplicate count. Allocation/admission failure falls back to the already-classified raw route. There is no fixed universal RAM ceiling. These payloads are not process peaks or minimum-RAM requirements. All12contexts observe the intended route and exact counts: primary sparse;2x2 original admitted grouping; OFF original query wrapper.\n\n## Complete-query latency\n\nAMD Ryzen9 9950X,16physical cores/32logical CPUs. Individually pinned custom Rayon workers use512KiB stacks: CPU2solo,0–15for16workers,0–31for32. Same driver bytes and canonical point/scalar fixtures for both coherent builds. Timers include global classification, optional preparation, point formation, ordinary/top fills, component/part merges, weighting, normalization, allocation/drop and pool dispatch/join. Exclusive B2 word reset is charged identically inside both timers. Loading, pool creation and independent oracle checks are excluded. Every timed result is checked afterward.\n\nFifteen fixed rotated/reversed paired blocks follow24preflights. Each latency process has3checked primes,20timed queries and1checked post. The360latency processes finish before separate diagnostics. No owned build/hash/archive/analysis workload overlaps capture; exclusive host use is not claimed.\n\n| Shape/query | Workers | OFF ms/query | Candidate ms/query | Paired candidate/OFF | Bounds | Wins/15 |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
 for shape in["1x2","2x2"]{for query in["A","B"]{for w in[1,16,32]{let c=&a["cases"][format!("{shape}-{query}-w{w}")];let q=&c["candidate_over_control"];writeln!(text,"|{shape}/{query}|{w}|{:.6}|{:.6}|{:.6}|[{:.6},{:.6}]|{}|",c["control_ns"]["median"].as_f64().unwrap()/1e6,c["candidate_ns"]["median"].as_f64().unwrap()/1e6,q["median"].as_f64().unwrap(),q["lower"].as_f64().unwrap(),q["upper"].as_f64().unwrap(),q["wins"]).unwrap();}}}
 text.push_str("\nGate requirements were declared before capture: primary equal-log A/B2 at32workers upper<1 and at least10wins, with every context upper≤1.03. The aggregate and three context bounds fail: primary A-w32, B-w16 and B-w32. Ranks4–12of15 provide nominal96.484375%marginal median intervals, without familywise coverage. Marginal time medians need not divide into paired estimates. No minimum gain floor, exclusions, extension, favorable subset, parameter sweep or unchanged rerun. The2x2 bypass observations measure separate linked wrappers/classifier code; their changes do not establish coalescing gains. No previously frozen complete-query caller exists for this diagnostic API, so OFF uses fresh original-source bodies; this is not accepted-SDK calibration.\n\n## CPU service, faults and process memory\n\n| Shape/query | Workers | OFF/candidate CPU ms/query | OFF/candidate peak RSS MiB | OFF/candidate median minor faults/query | OFF/candidate median voluntary switches/query |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
 for shape in["1x2","2x2"]{for query in["A","B"]{for w in[1,16,32]{let name=format!("{shape}-{query}-w{w}");let c=&a["cases"][&name];let median=|label:&str,k:&str|{let mut v=a["rows"].as_array().unwrap().iter().filter(|x|x["job"]["panel"]=="latency"&&x["job"]["case"]["name"]==name&&x["job"]["variant"]==label).map(|x|x["receipt"]["usage"][k].as_f64().unwrap()/20.).collect::<Vec<_>>();assert_eq!(v.len(),15);v.sort_by(f64::total_cmp);v[7]};writeln!(text,"|{shape}/{query}|{w}|{:.3}/{:.3}|{:.2}/{:.2}|{:.2}/{:.2}|{:.2}/{:.2}|",c["control_CPU_ms"]["median"].as_f64().unwrap(),c["candidate_CPU_ms"]["median"].as_f64().unwrap(),c["control_max_process_RSS_MiB"].as_f64().unwrap(),c["candidate_max_process_RSS_MiB"].as_f64().unwrap(),median("control","minor_faults"),median("candidate","minor_faults"),median("control","voluntary_switches"),median("candidate","voluntary_switches")).unwrap();}}}
 text.push_str("\nA reduces aggregate CPU service at32workers even as latency worsens. B2's CPU service is nearly unchanged while latency increases. Total work and high-core latency therefore move differently in this implementation. Additional serial preparation, sparse-span overhead, task shape and arithmetic batch changes are possible contributors; these observations do not isolate their causal costs. Faults are per-process minor faults over the warm timed loop, not swap reads or a RAM-capacity failure. RSS is process high-water sampled at loop end and includes loading/primes; it is not a complete prover peak, incremental overlay cost or minimumRAM. Changes are mixed, including the2x2 bypass.\n\n## Automatic warm counters and profiles\n\nAfter all latency:8FIFO-gated grouped stat processes, then8separate fixed16Mperiod cycles:u recordings, primary A/B2 at16/32workers,200timed queries after3excluded primes. All four grouped events qualify at100%reported coverage. These are descriptive panels, not paired counter confidence intervals.\n\n| Query/workers/arm | Cycles M/query | Instructions M/query | Generic cache misses M/query | Branch misses M/query |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
